write a function using pointer to exchange the values stored in two location in the memory 
#include <stdio.h>

void exchange(int *a, int *b);

int main()
{
    int x, y;

    x = 100;
    y = 200;

    printf("Before exchange: x = %d  y = %d\n", x, y);

    exchange(&x, &y);   /* function call */

    printf("After exchange: x = %d  y = %d\n", x, y);

    return 0;
}

void exchange(int *a, int *b)
{
    int t;

    t = *a;     /* store value of a */
    *a = *b;    /* put b into a */
    *b = t;     /* put t into b */
}
Explanation:
&x and &y → addresses of variables are passed
int *a, *b → pointer parameters receive addresses
*a → value stored at address a
*b → value stored at address b
Swapping process:
Plain text
x = 100
y = 200

t = *a = 100
*a = *b = 200
*b = t = 100
Output:
Plain text
Before exchange: x = 100  y = 200
After exchange: x = 200  y = 100